Removes the byte bound from `batch::batch_len` (§7.4, draft 34), so a
batch ends only at 10,000 entries however long their paths, and 10,000
entries of 4,096-byte paths come to some 42 MB, over §6.3's 16 MiB
frame. The simulator's paths are a few bytes long and its batches a few
hundred entries, so no seed reaches the bound and the rule's unit tests
stand in.

test: delocal-engine batch::tests::splits_long_paths_at_four_mebibytes
test: delocal-engine batch::tests::the_longest_paths_split_into_batches_that_each_fit_a_frame

diff --git a/crates/engine/src/batch.rs b/crates/engine/src/batch.rs
index c9c0d3e..98804d2 100644
--- a/crates/engine/src/batch.rs
+++ b/crates/engine/src/batch.rs
@@ -219,7 +219,7 @@ fn batch_len(records: &[(&IndexRecord, Option<ChangeKind>)]) -> usize {
     let mut bytes = 0;
     for (i, (record, _)) in records.iter().enumerate() {
         bytes += estimated_bytes(&record.entry);
-        if i == MAX_BATCH_ENTRIES || (i > 0 && bytes > MAX_BATCH_BYTES) {
+        if i == MAX_BATCH_ENTRIES {
             return i;
         }
     }
